237
00:13:56,370 --> 00:14:00,570
And it does this using
sulfatides, surface

238
00:14:00,570 --> 00:14:03,770
glycolipids, along with cord
factor, which is another

239
00:14:03,770 --> 00:14:09,260
surface glycolipid, and
that will not only prevent

240
00:14:09,260 --> 00:14:12,670
phagosome-lysosome fusion,
as I mentioned earlier,

241
00:14:12,770 --> 00:14:16,610
but will also trigger
signaling that helps to

242
00:14:16,610 --> 00:14:19,450
build tight clusters of
immune cells that wall

243
00:14:19,450 --> 00:14:23,070
off the infection and
promote granuloma formation.

244
00:14:27,450 --> 00:14:30,290
Mycobacterium tuberculosis
is the causative

245
00:14:30,290 --> 00:14:33,290
agent of the disease
tuberculosis, and

246
00:14:33,290 --> 00:14:35,650
humans are the natural
reservoir for it.

247
00:14:36,130 --> 00:14:38,930
Mycobacterium is also
extremely resistant

248
00:14:38,930 --> 00:14:41,950
to dehydration due
to its cell envelope

249
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:44,330
containing that mycolic
acid that we mentioned.

250
00:14:44,750 --> 00:14:46,710
This is going to
allow the bacteria to

251
00:14:46,710 --> 00:14:49,330
survive in the air,
and so it can remain

252
00:14:49,330 --> 00:14:53,450
infectious in dust or
droplet nuclei for days.

253
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:57,310
The bacteria is going to
be spread by droplets when

254
00:14:57,310 --> 00:15:01,410
infected people cough or
sneeze or speak, and the

255
00:15:01,410 --> 00:15:04,050
risk for infection is
going to be highest among

256
00:15:04,050 --> 00:15:07,810
socioeconomically
disadvantaged people, generally

257
00:15:07,810 --> 00:15:11,130
who have poor housing
conditions or poor nutrition.

258
00:15:11,530 --> 00:15:15,250
And this is why tuberculosis
is associated with

259
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:18,730
populations like prisoners
or the homeless, in

260
00:15:18,730 --> 00:15:21,070
addition to health care
workers that might come

261
00:15:21,070 --> 00:15:23,550
in frequent contact with
infected individuals.

262
00:15:24,130 --> 00:15:27,150
The likelihood that
infection will progress

263
00:15:27,150 --> 00:15:30,170
to active disease is
generally dependent on

264
00:15:30,170 --> 00:15:33,910
an individual's immune
competence. So the risk

265
00:15:33,910 --> 00:15:35,900
is going to be greater
for certain groups,

266
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:39,110
like individuals that
have HIV, for instance.

267
00:15:39,990 --> 00:15:43,910
Tuberculosis can involve
many organs, but the

268
00:15:43,910 --> 00:15:46,310
initial site of infection
is going to be the lungs.

269
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:49,890
And if the primary infection
heals without disease,

270
00:15:49,890 --> 00:15:52,850
then it's considered to
be a latent infection.

271
00:15:52,850 --> 00:15:55,590
And about 90% of
infections will become

272
00:15:55,590 --> 00:15:58,050
latent instead of
having active disease.

273
00:15:58,330 --> 00:16:01,450
And for those with
latent infection, a small

274
00:16:01,450 --> 00:16:04,930
percentage of about 10%
will have reactivation at a

275
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:07,690
later time and then
progress to active disease.

276
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:11,350
Now, during active disease,
you would expect to see

277
00:16:11,350 --> 00:16:14,330
constitutional symptoms
with the potential for

278
00:16:14,330 --> 00:16:17,050
coughing up blood if it's
progressed significantly.

279
00:16:20,910 --> 00:16:24,950
The pathogenesis of
tuberculosis begins when the

280
00:16:24,950 --> 00:16:28,590
bacteria is inhaled into
the lungs, which would then

281
00:16:28,590 --> 00:16:32,550
be engulfed by alveolar
macrophages and then

282
00:16:32,550 --> 00:16:36,150
ultimately evade destruction,
as we mentioned before,

283
00:16:36,270 --> 00:16:39,210
allowing them to then
replicate inside the cell.

284
00:16:39,310 --> 00:16:42,930
Now, this is going to trigger
an immune response, and

285
00:16:42,930 --> 00:16:46,430
that will begin to form
a protective granuloma,

286
00:16:46,430 --> 00:16:49,870
which is sometimes called a
caseating granuloma because

287
00:16:49,870 --> 00:16:53,740
the infection often
creates an area of central

288
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:58,270
necrosis that has the look
of soft cheese to it. Now,

289
00:16:58,270 --> 00:17:00,490
that's surrounded by a
ring of immune cells,

290
00:17:00,490 --> 00:17:05,310
including macrophages and
lymphocytes. And it's the T

291
00:17:05,310 --> 00:17:08,010
-cells in the area that
will then begin to secrete

292
00:17:08,010 --> 00:17:11,510
interferon gamma, which
could be used diagnostically.

293
00:17:12,050 --> 00:17:16,250
Now, the primary tuberculosis
lesion in the lung,

294
00:17:16,250 --> 00:17:20,190
forming that dead tissue
area, the caseating

295
00:17:20,190 --> 00:17:24,650
granuloma, is also called
the GON focus. And that

296
00:17:24,650 --> 00:17:27,650
could be combined with
infection in the nearby lymph

297
00:17:27,650 --> 00:17:30,490
nodes to make what's
known as the GAN complex.

298
00:17:30,750 --> 00:17:34,190
Now, oftentimes, the
granulomas become walled off by

299
00:17:34,190 --> 00:17:38,850
fibrous tissue and can
eventually calcify over time if

300
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:41,830
the immune system successfully
controls the bacteria.

301
00:17:42,030 --> 00:17:44,850
But the bacteria can
also remain dormant

302
00:17:44,850 --> 00:17:48,030
at this stage and can
reactivate later if

303
00:17:48,030 --> 00:17:50,050
a person's immunologic
responsiveness

304
00:17:50,050 --> 00:17:53,470
changes with age or
for some other reason.

305
00:17:54,390 --> 00:17:55,870
Now, like you see
in the picture,

306
00:17:56,090 --> 00:18:01,450
about 10% of infection
will continue to progress,

307
00:18:01,450 --> 00:18:04,810
and that's a concern
for high-risk patients.

308
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:08,360
Generally, the infection
will spread to the upper

309
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:10,750
lobes in the lungs, and
that provides a more

310
00:18:10,750 --> 00:18:13,610
oxygen-rich environment
for the aerobic bacteria.

311
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:16,310
And this is going to be
a continuation of the

312
00:18:16,310 --> 00:18:18,990
initial infection if it
fails to be controlled

313
00:18:18,990 --> 00:18:22,930
by the immune system.
So there's There's no

314
00:18:22,930 --> 00:18:25,910
latency here, and this
would also be contagious.

315
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:31,390
Now either the primary
progressive or reactivation

316
00:18:31,390 --> 00:18:34,890
can potentially lead to
extra pulmonary tuberculosis,

317
00:18:35,210 --> 00:18:37,940
which is also known as
miliary tuberculosis.

318
00:18:38,090 --> 00:18:40,070
And this is going to
be where the bacteria

319
00:18:40,070 --> 00:18:42,390
travel through the
blood and then can

320
00:18:42,390 --> 00:18:45,030
begin to affect
alternate sites in the

321
00:18:45,030 --> 00:18:48,830
body, like the bones,
kidney, or the brain.

322
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:57,290
Diagnosing tuberculosis
is usually based on either

323
00:18:57,290 --> 00:19:02,430
the tuberculin skin test or
an interferon gamma assay.

324
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:07,410
The purified protein
derivative PPD skin test

325
00:19:07,410 --> 00:19:10,270
leverages proteins that
are associated with

326
00:19:10,270 --> 00:19:15,090
the bacterial cell wall
and elicit a delayed type

327
00:19:15,090 --> 00:19:18,710
4 hypersensitivity
reaction, which requires

328
00:19:18,710 --> 00:19:22,430
prior sensitization to
tuberculosis antigens.

329
00:19:23,390 --> 00:19:26,370
Then, by measuring
the duration of the

330
00:19:26,370 --> 00:19:28,810
injection site, you
can identify either

331
00:19:28,810 --> 00:19:31,530
active or latent
infection at that point.

332
00:19:31,530 --> 00:19:34,930
And the size that's
required for a positive test

333
00:19:34,930 --> 00:19:38,690
changes with the specific
group. So the size would

334
00:19:38,690 --> 00:19:42,650
only have to reach about
5 millimeters for somebody

335
00:19:42,650 --> 00:19:46,330
who's immunosuppressed,
but it would have to be as

336
00:19:46,330 --> 00:19:49,390
large as 15 millimeters
for someone with low risk.

337
00:19:50,020 --> 00:19:52,810
Now, the skin test will
also give a positive

338
00:19:52,810 --> 00:19:55,490
result, which would be
a false positive due

339
00:19:55,490 --> 00:20:00,110
to BCG vaccination,
which is a vaccine that

340
00:20:00,110 --> 00:20:04,370
is a live attenuated
vaccine that uses a

341
00:20:04,370 --> 00:20:07,570
related species known
as Mycobacterium bovis.

342
00:20:08,530 --> 00:20:12,960
now there's also the
interferon gamma release assay

343
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:17,170
as an alternative to the
skin test and that uses

344
00:20:17,170 --> 00:20:21,090
immunoassays to measure
interferon gamma production

345
00:20:21,090 --> 00:20:25,550
by sensitized t-cells
that are stimulated by

346
00:20:25,550 --> 00:20:30,470
mycobacterium tuberculosis
antigens so if a person had

347
00:20:30,470 --> 00:20:34,210
prior exposure the antigens
will cause interferon

348
00:20:34,210 --> 00:20:37,550
gamma production from T
-cells in a blood sample.

349
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:40,130
Now, this is beneficial
when you have a patient

350
00:20:40,130 --> 00:20:43,110
who might give a false
skin test reaction,

351
00:20:43,310 --> 00:20:46,330
like somebody who
had the BCG vaccine

352
00:20:46,330 --> 00:20:48,050
and would give a
false positive,

353
00:20:48,690 --> 00:20:53,290
or a patient who's
immunosuppressed and could

354
00:20:53,290 --> 00:20:56,390
possibly give a false
negative skin reaction test.

355
00:20:57,110 --> 00:21:00,810
now if either test is
positive you still don't

356
00:21:00,810 --> 00:21:05,450
necessarily know if the
patient has active or latent TB

357
00:21:05,450 --> 00:21:10,390
so from there you could
stain a sputum sample like

358
00:21:10,390 --> 00:21:13,290
we mentioned before and
try to culture the bacteria

359
00:21:14,310 --> 00:21:17,110
there's always an option to
run a PCR which is another

360
00:21:17,110 --> 00:21:21,650
diagnostic tool but
evidence with imaging will

361
00:21:21,650 --> 00:21:25,310
also be helpful here to
show active infection if you

362
00:21:25,310 --> 00:21:28,290
were to see holes or air
spaces in the upper parts

363
00:21:28,290 --> 00:21:31,650
of the lungs or any sort of
inflammation or pneumonia

364
00:21:31,650 --> 00:21:36,110
like damage or if you
see tiny scattered spots

365
00:21:36,110 --> 00:21:39,810
that look like millet seeds
across the lungs like you

366
00:21:39,810 --> 00:21:43,090
could see in the image
here on the slide and that

367
00:21:43,090 --> 00:21:47,210
would indicate miliary
tuberculosis showing that the

368
00:21:47,210 --> 00:21:49,990
infection has disseminated
and could lead to

369
00:21:49,990 --> 00:21:54,200
complications in other parts
of the body, like meningitis,

370
00:21:54,250 --> 00:21:58,190
pericarditis, or POTS
disease, which is spinal

371
00:21:58,190 --> 00:22:01,930
tuberculosis that damages
the vertebrae and the discs.

372
00:22:05,930 --> 00:22:09,490
Okay, the treatment plan
is typically a multi

373
00:22:09,490 --> 00:22:12,670
-drug therapy, which is
used for a decently long

374
00:22:12,670 --> 00:22:16,390
period of time and it
also depends on the stage.

375
00:22:16,830 --> 00:22:20,110
So for latent
infection rifampin or

376
00:22:20,110 --> 00:22:24,630
isoniazid is used
and for active TB a

377
00:22:24,630 --> 00:22:27,150
treatment plan of four
drugs would be used.

378
00:22:27,690 --> 00:22:30,510
Now of course this doesn't
account for any particular

379
00:22:30,510 --> 00:22:33,810
isolate that might be
resistant to one or more of

380
00:22:33,810 --> 00:22:37,190
the drugs since there are
some strains of tuberculosis

381
00:22:37,190 --> 00:22:40,590
that are classified as
extensively drug resistant.

382
00:22:44,510 --> 00:22:47,850
Okay, so the last
of this group is a

383
00:22:47,850 --> 00:22:50,750
bacterium called
Burkholderia cepatia.

384
00:22:51,570 --> 00:22:55,390
Now, Burkholderia actually
used to be part of the

385
00:22:55,390 --> 00:22:58,690
genus Pseudomonas, but
that was changed about 30

386
00:22:58,690 --> 00:23:03,170
years ago based on more
modern genetic analysis.

387
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:06,950
Now, as such, you would
expect many of its

388
00:23:06,950 --> 00:23:10,750
characteristics to be similar
to Pseudomonas, So it can

389
00:23:10,750 --> 00:23:14,170
colonize a variety of moist
environmental surfaces,

390
00:23:14,170 --> 00:23:17,510
and it can act as an
opportunistic pathogen.

391
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:22,410
So people with cystic
fibrosis, chronic lung

392
00:23:22,410 --> 00:23:26,430
disease, or weakened immune
defenses are particularly

393
00:23:26,430 --> 00:23:30,610
susceptible to it. And
again, like Pseudomonas,

394
00:23:30,610 --> 00:23:32,970
it's resistant to many
common antibiotics.

395
00:23:36,510 --> 00:23:39,270
All right, in this last
section, we're going

396
00:23:39,270 --> 00:23:42,170
to talk about the
zoonotic and occupational

397
00:23:42,170 --> 00:23:44,590
bacterial exposures
that can lead to

398
00:23:44,590 --> 00:23:47,210
infections involving
the respiratory system.

399
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:54,190
So we'll start here
with coxiella, which is

400
00:23:54,190 --> 00:23:56,110
a microbe that we've
talked about before.

401
00:23:56,510 --> 00:24:00,390
It's an obligate intracellular
pathogen, and because

402
00:24:00,390 --> 00:24:03,070
of that, it's considered
to be rickettsia-like.

403
00:24:03,570 --> 00:24:07,030
Coxiella and rickettsia
are both small,

404
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:09,290
gram-negative,
intracellular bacterial

405
00:24:09,290 --> 00:24:12,330
pathogens that rely
on host cells to live,

406
00:24:12,810 --> 00:24:15,030
and were historically
grouped together

407
00:24:15,030 --> 00:24:17,490
because of these shared
traits that they have,

408
00:24:17,490 --> 00:24:20,330
but Coxiella is now
classified separately.

409
00:24:20,470 --> 00:24:23,670
A major distinction
is transmission.

410
00:24:24,390 --> 00:24:27,890
rickettsia is mainly spread
by an arthropod vector

411
00:24:27,890 --> 00:24:31,430
while coxiella is
typically spread through

412
00:24:31,430 --> 00:24:34,970
airborne dust and aerosols
from infected animals

413
00:24:34,970 --> 00:24:41,310
like cattle sheep and
goats it's found in very

414
00:24:41,310 --> 00:24:44,730
high concentrations in
those animals feces urine

415
00:24:44,730 --> 00:24:48,310
and even in placental tissue
and can be transmitted

416
00:24:48,310 --> 00:24:51,670
to humans by aerosol
from those materials.

417
00:24:51,790 --> 00:24:54,350
It could also be contracted by

418
00:24:54,350 --> 00:24:56,490
consuming unpasteurized
milk as well.

419
00:24:57,450 --> 00:24:59,830
Now coxiella is a bit
different from other

420
00:24:59,830 --> 00:25:02,430
bacteria because it
undergoes a phase

421
00:25:02,430 --> 00:25:05,690
transition where its
LPS structure changes

422
00:25:05,690 --> 00:25:09,290
which then impacts the
disease progression.

423
00:25:10,070 --> 00:25:14,410
It also forms tough
spore-like structures

424
00:25:14,410 --> 00:25:17,290
that you can see in the
image on the slide and

425
00:25:17,290 --> 00:25:19,430
those can persist in
the environment for

426
00:25:19,430 --> 00:25:21,930
years because they're
resistant to drying out.

427
00:25:21,930 --> 00:25:25,570
Though it's not exactly
like a true endospore like

428
00:25:25,570 --> 00:25:28,590
you would find in a
clostridium species or a

429
00:25:28,590 --> 00:25:31,390
bacillus species like we'll
see in our next example.

430
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:36,130
The disease it causes
is called Q fever,

431
00:25:36,130 --> 00:25:38,210
which stands for
query because it was

432
00:25:38,210 --> 00:25:40,690
unidentified during
its initial outbreak.

433
00:25:40,990 --> 00:25:43,750
It typically presents
with mild flu-like

434
00:25:43,750 --> 00:25:46,670
symptoms, but it can
also present with more

435
00:25:46,670 --> 00:25:49,650
severe fever, hepatitis,
and pneumonia.

436
00:25:50,670 --> 00:25:54,930
Chronic fever can occur
years after initial exposure,

437
00:25:54,930 --> 00:25:58,950
but generally only in those
patients with predisposing

438
00:25:58,950 --> 00:26:02,190
factors like immune
suppression or heart issues

439
00:26:02,190 --> 00:26:05,930
where it might manifest
in subacute endocarditis.

440
00:26:10,210 --> 00:26:14,630
Bacillus anthracis causes
the disease anthrax, which

441
00:26:14,630 --> 00:26:17,930
is common in animals but
relatively rare in humans.

442
00:26:18,410 --> 00:26:22,970
Bacillus is a non-modal,
aerobic, gram-positive

443
00:26:22,970 --> 00:26:26,110
bacterium that is
frequently seen in chains

444
00:26:26,110 --> 00:26:29,950
as a streptobacillus
and is also a known

445
00:26:29,950 --> 00:26:33,210
endospore former when
nutrients are limited.

446
00:26:33,850 --> 00:26:35,970
As you can see in
the image, the spores

447
00:26:35,970 --> 00:26:38,450
are generated centrally
within the cells.

448
00:26:38,530 --> 00:26:41,870
The spores also lack
any sort of metabolic

449
00:26:41,870 --> 00:26:44,430
activity, and they're
highly resistant to

450
00:26:44,430 --> 00:26:46,910
chemicals and other
environmental stresses.

451
00:26:47,730 --> 00:26:51,250
The bacterial cells will
also produce a capsule

452
00:26:51,250 --> 00:26:55,350
that's antiphagocytic and
composed of glutamate,

453
00:26:55,350 --> 00:26:57,590
which is kind of unique
because bacterial

454
00:26:57,590 --> 00:27:00,930
capsules are usually
polysaccharide-based instead.

455
00:27:01,950 --> 00:27:06,250
Now, the anthrax toxin that
it produces is actually

456
00:27:06,250 --> 00:27:11,310
two exotoxins called edema
factor and lethal factor.

457
00:27:11,450 --> 00:27:15,690
The first of those is an
adenylate cyclase, which

458
00:27:15,690 --> 00:27:19,290
is going to increase the
levels of cyclic AMP in the

459
00:27:19,290 --> 00:27:23,210
cell and thereby causes
fluid to leave the cells.

460
00:27:23,470 --> 00:27:26,370
And the other one
is a protease that's

461
00:27:26,370 --> 00:27:28,770
going to stop MAP
kinase signaling, which

462
00:27:28,770 --> 00:27:31,690
ultimately leads to
cell death and necrosis.

463
00:27:32,470 --> 00:27:36,730
Now, the two exotoxins are
classified as AB toxins.

464
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:40,110
And what that means is
that they have a binding

465
00:27:40,110 --> 00:27:44,190
subunit, and those binding
subunits are called

466
00:27:44,190 --> 00:27:47,230
protective antigen. Now,
this is kind of a misnomer

467
00:27:47,230 --> 00:27:52,310
since the binding subunit
allows for the catalytic

468
00:27:52,310 --> 00:27:55,390
enzymatic subunit into
the cell, but it's

469
00:27:55,390 --> 00:27:58,530
called protective because
antibodies raised against

470
00:27:58,530 --> 00:28:02,310
those B binding subunits
will prevent disease.

471
00:28:07,010 --> 00:28:10,090
Anthrax in humans can
occur in three different

472
00:28:10,090 --> 00:28:14,270
forms, cutaneous, pulmonary,
or gastrointestinal.

473
00:28:14,570 --> 00:28:18,230
In the cutaneous form,
the typical lesion

474
00:28:18,230 --> 00:28:20,770
is a painless ulcer
with a black crust

475
00:28:20,770 --> 00:28:22,990
on it, like you can
see in the image.

476
00:28:23,370 --> 00:28:26,190
This form infrequently
progresses,

477
00:28:26,190 --> 00:28:29,090
so without antibiotic
treatment, death is

478
00:28:29,090 --> 00:28:31,570
only going to occur in
a minority of cases,

479
00:28:31,730 --> 00:28:34,050
somewhere around
10 to 20 percent.

480
00:28:34,790 --> 00:28:37,790
The pulmonary form
of anthrax, which

481
00:28:37,790 --> 00:28:39,870
is also known as Wool
-Sorter's disease,

482
00:28:40,030 --> 00:28:43,550
usually begins with
nonspecific flu-like symptoms,

